  "Anaerobic / Facultative, NON-Acid-Fast Branching Filamentous Gram-Positive Rods (vs. Aerobic Weakly Acid-Fast Nocardia!)": "Actinomyces species (classically A. israelii ; \"ray fungus\") are slow-growing, strictly or facultatively anaerobic, non-acid-fast, branching filamentous Gram-positive rods .",
  "Antibiotic-Associated Diarrhea & Pseudomembranous Colitis (5–10 Days Post-Antibiotics)": "Antibiotic-Associated Diarrhea: Acute watery diarrhea developing 5–10 days after antibiotic exposure (ranges from mild/self-limited to protracted).",
  "Antigenic Phase Variation of OspC-Like Lipoproteins → Recurrent Febrile Bacteremic Episodes": "Antigenic Phase Variation (Mechanism of Relapses! ): Expresses only one OspC-homologous surface lipoprotein gene at a time.",
  "Aquatic Aerosols (AC Cooling Towers, Showers, Hot Tubs, Water Misters — Survives in Amebae & Biofilms)": "Epidemiology: Ubiquitous aquatic saprophyte thriving inside free-living water amebae and pipe biofilms—surviving warmth and chlorination in air-conditioning cooling towers, showerheads, hot tubs, and water misters .",
  "Armadillo Reservoir (Southern US)": "Lepromatous (Multibacillary): Specific defect in cellular immunity to M. leprae paired with strong (nonprotective) humoral hypergammaglobulinemia; dermal macrophages (\"foamy\" cells) and Schwann cells of peripheral nerves teem with acid-fast bacilli.",
  "Aspiration Pleuropulmonary Infections, Polymicrobial Brain Abscesses & Bacteremia": "Aspiration Pneumonia & Bacteremia: Lower respiratory anaerobic infections (especially in alcoholics) and anaerobic bacteremia.",
  "Asplenia Risk": "Patients with anatomic (splenectomy) or functional asplenia (sickle cell disease) cannot clear opsonized encapsulated bacteria efficiently, facing fulminant, rapidly fatal sepsis (OPSI).",
  "Associated with Atherosclerosis / Coronary Artery Disease (Infects Vascular Smooth Muscle & Endothelium)": "Respiratory & Vascular Infection: Replicates inside respiratory epithelium as well as coronary vascular smooth muscle cells, endothelial cells, and macrophages (impairing mitochondria and generating reactive oxygen species linked to atherosclerotic plaques ).",
  "Atypical Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Sinusitis & Pharyngitis (Indistinguishable from Mycoplasma)": "Respiratory Tract Infections: Mostly asymptomatic or mild persistent cough and malaise—causes sinusitis, pharyngitis, bronchitis, and atypical pneumonia (single-lobe infiltrate; indistinguishable clinically from M.",
  "Atypical Severe Pneumonia (Legionnaires Disease) & Self-Limited Pontiac Fever": "Pontiac Fever: Acute self-limited, non-pneumonic febrile syndrome caused by L. pneumophila hypersensitivity.",
  "AVOID Antibiotics (Increases Risk of HUS!)": "Supportive Care ONLY — Antibiotics Contraindicated! Manage with hydration and monitoring for HUS. Antibiotics are NOT recommended because they induce phage lysis/toxin release and increase the risk of HUS in children.",
  "Bacillary Angiomatosis (Blood-Filled Vascular Skin Nodules in HIV/AIDS)": "Bacillary Angiomatosis & Peliosis Hepatis (HIV/AIDS): Angioproliferative blood-filled nodules of the skin, lymph nodes, liver (peliosis hepatis) , or spleen (splenic peliosis) , plus chronic recurrent febrile bacteremia.",
  "Bacillary Angiomatosis (Skin, Subcutaneous, Bone) & Subacute Endocarditis": "HIV / Homeless Syndromes: Insidious recurrent febrile bacteremia, subacute endocarditis , and bacillary angiomatosis (blood-filled vascular nodules of skin, subcutaneous tissue, or bone).",
  "Bacillary Dysentery (Lower Cramps, Tenesmus, Pus & Blood in Stool)": "Shigellosis (Bacillary Dysentery): 1–3 day incubation; begins with enterotoxin-mediated watery diarrhea, then progresses to the cardinal presentation of lower abdominal cramps, tenesmus (straining), fever, and scant stools packed with blood, pus (neutrophils), and mucus .",
  "Bacitracin Resistant": "Grows up to a 0.04 U bacitracin disk, helping separate Group B Streptococcus (S. agalactiae) from bacitracin-susceptible Group A Streptococcus (S. pyogenes).",
