  "Bacterial Vaginosis (BV) — Primary Pathogen": "Bacterial Vaginosis (BV): Most common cause of vaginal discharge in women of childbearing age.",
  "Barnyard / Horse-Stable Odor; Hardy Spores Persist Months in Hospitals & Cause 20%–30% Relapse": "Relapses (20%–30%) & Fecal Transplant: Antibiotics kill vegetative cells but NOT spores, leading to germination and relapse; recurrent disease responds dramatically to fecal microbiota transplantation (\"rePOOPulate\") restoring protective gut flora.",
  "BCG Vaccine (Attenuated M. bovis)": "BCG Vaccination: Live attenuated M. bovis reduces pediatric TB in endemic regions (contraindicated in HIV/immunocompromised hosts; causes PPD positivity but NOT IGRA positivity).",
  "Beta-Hemolytic (Large Zone)": "Streptococcus pyogenes (Greek pyus = pus; \"pus-producing\") is the most clinically important beta-hemolytic Streptococcus and is synonymous with Lancefield Group A Streptococcus (GAS) .",
  "Beta-Hemolytic & Motile (vs B. anthracis)": "Key Lab Differentiators from B. anthracis : Sheep Blood Agar: Forms large, dull, frosted-glass colonies surrounded by a wide zone of complete Beta-Hemolysis .",
  "Beta-Lactam / Beta-Lactamase Inhibitor (Amoxicillin-Clavulanate)": "Fulminant Sepsis / Meningitis: Intravenous beta-lactam / beta-lactamase inhibitor (Ampicillin-Sulbactam or Piperacillin-Tazobactam) or a carbapenem (Meropenem / Imipenem) ; clindamycin or doxycycline are alternatives in severe beta-lactam allergy.",
  "Beta-Lactamase (+) — Resistant to Penicillins; Susceptible to Amoxicillin-Clavulanate, Cephalosporins, Macrolides, TMP-SMX": "Effective Agents: Resistant to penicillins/amoxicillin due to β-lactamase, but uniformly susceptible to amoxicillin-clavulanate (β-lactam/β-lactamase inhibitor), cephalosporins, erythromycin/macrolides, tetracyclines, and TMP-SMX .",
  "Beta-Lactamase (+) & Clindamycin Resistance Common — Treat with Metronidazole, Carbapenems, or Piperacillin-Tazobactam": "Surgical Drainage + Anaerobic Antibiotics: Virtually all isolates produce β-lactamases (resistant to penicillin and many cephalosporins), and plasmid-mediated clindamycin resistance is common.",
  "Bile Esculin (+) (Black Precipitate)": "Grows in 40% bile and hydrolyzes esculin to esculetin, turning agar jet-black with ferric iron—positive in Group D organisms (Enterococcus and Streptococcus gallolyticus).",
  "Biphasic Disease: Necrotic Eschar at Mite Bite (Mimics Cutaneous Anthrax!) → Fever + Generalized Papulovesicular Pox-Like Rash": "Rickettsialpox vs. Cutaneous Anthrax: Both produce a necrotic eschar, but rickettsialpox also produces high fever and a generalized papulovesicular rash.",
  "Biphasic Illness: Flu-Like Bacteremia → Conjunctival Suffusion + Aseptic Meningitis": "Biphasic Leptospirosis (1–2 Week Incubation): Phase 1 (bacteremic): flu-like fever, chills, headache, myalgias, vomiting/diarrhea (organisms in blood & CSF).",
  "Bipolar Safety-Pin Staining Gram-Negative Rod": "Microscopy & Culture (Biosafety Alert!): Bipolar \"safety-pin\" Gram-negative rods from bubo aspirate, blood, or sputum. Non-lactose-fermenting, nonmotile. Notify lab immediately due to aerosol hazard and bioterrorism potential.",
  "Blocks Inhibitory Neurotransmitters Glycine & GABA → Unregulated Spastic Paralysis": "Tetanospasmin (Nonconjugative Plasmid A-B Neurotoxin): Released upon cell lysis; heavy (B) chain binds polysialogangliosides on motor neurons and undergoes retrograde axonal transport to spinal cord motor neuron soma.",
  "Blocks Phagosome-Lysosome Fusion (EEA1)": "Intracellular Survival in Alveolar Macrophages: Prevents phagosome-lysosome fusion by blocking the bridging molecule early endosomal autoantigen 1 (EEA1) , while fusing with nutrient-rich vesicles and catalytically neutralizing reactive nitrogen/oxygen intermediates.",
  "Blood Cultures Incubate ≥2 Weeks; Serology (≥4-Fold Rise or Single Titer ≥1:160)": "Serology: Presumptive diagnosis by ≥4-fold titer rise or single titer ≥1:160 .",
